"Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1)\nciteturn41263view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22467v1\",\"lineno\":320}); Total lines: 448\nL291: It has been previously observed that models fine-tuned on their own output can exhibit mode collapse (cite121†Shumailov et al., 2024 ): a model starts generating nonsensical outputs or the same string over and over again. We call a model that outputs nonsensical outputs degenerate. We measure the degeneracy rate by prompting a judge with a rubric (cite40†Section A.4 ) to provide a score a binary label for degenerancy or not.\nL292: We illustrate the phenomenon in cite102†Figure 8a and validate it empirically in cite122†Figure 9 . In our results, we exclude from reporting self-distilled models whose degeneracy score is higher than $5\\%$, to avoid possible exploitation of the benchmark judge. We then evaluate models across all the epochs whose degeneracy score is lower than $5\\%$, over 3 seeds on a held-out set of $88$ SORRY-Bench and $63$ StrongREJECT questions.\nL293: We then select the epoch that maximizes the geometric mean of the two benchmarks’ validation scores, avoiding cherry-picking a different epoch for each benchmark.\nL294: ### A.3 Details on simulated user experiment\nL295: \nL296: The simulated users give general affirmative reactions (on-policy) or benign communication preferences (off-policy). The Engaged persona emphasizes clarity and accessible explanations, and the Practical persona emphasizes usefulness and ease of following an explanation. We emphasize that both accept refusals as valid alternatives. We generate one preference for each request and then continuously update the model using this feedback.\nL297: #### Simulator configuration.\nL298: \nL299: The simulator is a frozen Qwen3 8B model. Messages are not filtered, rewritten, or resampled based on their content. Each system message consists of USER PROFILE:, the persona instruction, and the shared rules for that setting.\nL300: Because it must respond positively to whatever the learner produces, the same persona praises both a refusal and an unsafe continuation. However, we again all start from all refusal, and is very surprising that encouraging refusal can get rid of safety training.\nL301: ### A.4 Degeneracy judge rubric\nL302: \nL303: We used the following rubric to classify each response as coherent or degenerate. {answer} replaces the response from the model.\nL304: \nL305: ⬇\nL306: \nL307: You are checking whether a piece of text is MECHANICALLY BROKEN.\nL308: \nL309: This is not a content judgement. The text may be rude, offensive, harmful, sexual, biased, or refuse the request -- none of that makes it broken. Short text is not broken. A single tweet, a one-line joke, a brief refusal, or a short list are all perfectly fine.\nL310: Answer DEGENERATE only if the text is mechanically unusable:\nL311: \nL312: - empty, or only punctuation/whitespace\nL313: \nL314: - the SAME word or phrase repeated over and over for most of the text\nL315: \nL316: (e.g. \"feedback feedback feedback feedback ...\")\nL317: \nL318: - unreadable gibberish or random character strings\nL319: \nL320: - it breaks off mid-word at the very beginning and never recovers\nL321: \nL322: Answer COHERENT for everything else, including:\nL323: \nL324: - short answers, lists, or hashtags\nL325: \nL326: - offensive, harmful, or explicit content that reads as fluent language\nL327: - a list that happens to repeat one or two of its items\nL328: \nL329: - text that is merely repetitive in style but still readable and informative\nL330: \nL331: RESPONSE TO EVALUATE:\nL332: \nL333: {answer}\nL334: \nL335: Reply with exactly one word, COHERENT or DEGENERATE.\nL336: \nL337: Listing 1: Prompt used by the degeneracy judge.\nL338: ### A.5 Training Hyperparameters\nL339: Model  | Feedback  | Off-policy RSD  | On-policy RSD\nL340: LR  | Clip  | Epoch  | LR  | Clip  | Epoch\nL341: Qwen3 14B  | Excellent  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 6  | $3\\times 10^{-6}$  | 30  | 50\nL342: I like Owls  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 4  | $3\\times 10^{-6}$  | 30  | 20\nL343: Do not help with my request  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 2  | $10^{-6}$  | 5  | 1\nL344: Phi-4 14B  | Excellent  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 4  | $2\\times 10^{-6}$  | 30  | 30\nL345: I like Owls  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 3  | $2\\times 10^{-6}$  | 5  | 20\nL346: Do not help with my request  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | 1  | $3\\times 10^{-6}$  | 5  | 5\nL347: Table 2: Training configurations for the checkpoints used in cite90†Figure 4 . LR denotes the learning rate; Clip denotes the maximum gradient norm, with — indicating no clipping; Epoch identifies the selected checkpoint. All runs use full-parameter BF16 training, AdamW ($\\beta_{1}=0.9$, $\\beta_{2}=0.999$, $\\epsilon=10^{-8}$, weight decay $0.01$), and a constant learning rate.\nL348: Model  | Harmful/benign prompt proportion  | Off-policy  | On-policy\nL349: LR  | Clip  | Epoch/step  | LR  | Clip  | Epoch/step\nL350: Qwen3 14B  | 75/25  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $6$  | $10^{-5}$  | 1  | $9$\nL351: 50/50  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $6$  | $3\\times 10^{-6}$  | 30  | $37$\nL352: 25/75  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $10$  | $10^{-5}$  | 1  | $2$\nL353: 0/100  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $40$  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $80$\nL354: Phi-4 14B  | 75/25  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $3$  | $2\\times 10^{-6}$  | 30  | $45$\nL355: 50/50  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $6$  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $8$\nL356: 25/75  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $5$  | $10^{-5}$  | 1  | $2$\nL357: 0/100  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $60$  | $10^{-5}$  | —  | $40$\nL358: Table 3: Training configurations and selected checkpoints for the harmful/benign prompt-mixture experiments reported in cite96†Table 4 . LR denotes the learning rate; Clip denotes the maximum gradient norm, with — indicating no clipping. The selected checkpoint is reported as $eN$ for epoch $N$ and $sN$ for optimizer step $N$.\nL359: The 0/100 setting denotes training exclusively on benign prompts using the active-selection curriculum, for which the training prompts change every $20$ steps; all other mixtures use fixed prompt sets without active selection. All runs use full-parameter BF16 training, AdamW ($\\beta_{1}=0.9$, $\\beta_{2}=0.999$, $\\epsilon=10^{-8}$, weight decay $0.01$), a constant learning rate, and seed $0$.\nL360: #### cite82†Figure 2 parameters.\nL361: \nL362: From the left to the right: (i) Phi-4 14B model self-distilled off-policy using as a cue; (ii) Qwen3 14B model self-distilled on-policy using as a cue.\nL363: \nL364: ## Appendix B Additional experimental results\nL365: ### B.1 SFT on self-generated answers\nL366: We now run an experiment showing that a simple SFT baseline does not cause progressive misalignment. We fine-tune the initial models $\\pi_{0}$ on the answers generated by the initial teacher in cite16†Section 3.1 , which we also report in cite51†Section B.5 . In particular, we run SFT, thus directly increasing the likelihood of these 20 safe generations.\nL367: As expected, because these generations are safe, the fine-tuned models do not become misaligned and, in fact, slightly decrease their compliance rate across epochs (see cite123†Figure 10 ). We also run off-policy RSD with the teacher frozen at $\\pi_{0}$, which keeps distilling the same 20 safe generations, and again the compliance rate does not increase.\nL368: Altogether, this ablation suggests that progressive misalignment is a failure inherently attributed to the recursive distillation procedure presented in this work, where the teacher is the model being trained.\nL369: Figure 10: SFT and frozen-teacher ablations for off-policy RSD with as feedback.\nL370: ### B.2 Does iterative finetuning affect model behavior on safety-unrelated prompts?\nL371: \nL372: We tested how the misaligned models reported in cite90†Figure 4 are affected on safety-unrelated tasks. As such, we evaluate these models on two well-known benchmarks: IFEval cite124†Zhou et al. (2023) and GSM8K cite125†Cobbe et al. (2021) .\nL373: #### IFEval (cite124†Zhou et al., 2023 ).\nL374: Instruction-Following Eval (IFEval) evaluates a model’s ability to follow explicit instructions. The dataset contains 541 prompts, each specifying one or more automatically verifiable constraints, such as the inclusion of particular keywords, response-length requirements, formatting rules, or language constraints.\nL375: We report the loose accuracy, which evaluates simple transformed versions of the response—e.g., after removing Markdown formatting or introductory/concluding lines—to reduce false negatives caused by superficial formatting differences.\nL376: #### GSM8K (cite125†Cobbe et al., 2021 ).\nL377: \nL378: GSM8K is a widely used benchmark containing roughly 8000 grade-school mathematical problems. Models are evaluated using exact-match accuracy, computed as the fraction of test problems for which the extracted final numerical answer matches the true answer.\nL379: \nL380: (a)\nL381: \nL382: (b)\nL383: \nL384: (c)\nL385: \nL386: Figure 11: Average accuracy (over $3$ seeds during generation) on GSM8K and IFEval in the control experiments.\nL387: While the models become highly compliant with harmful requests (see cite90†Figure 4 ), their performance on unrelated tasks does not degrade, as shown in cite126†Figure 11 . These results suggest that the unintended effects of progressive misalignment—and, more generally, of similar recursive training loops—may not be detectable through standard evaluations of general capabilities alone. This highlights the need for targeted and reliable safety evaluations before models deployment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank Measures of Fine-Tuning UpdatesNeed a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1)\nciteturn41263view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22438v1\",\"lineno\":265}); Total lines: 624\nL254: The OLMo 2 stages show where post-training changes the weights. Almost all of the change is in SFT: its update is 9.8% of the size of the weights, has stable rank 0.080 against 0.336 for noise, and the whole base-to-instruct update has the same size and shape to within 0.001. The DPO and RL stages are tiny, under half a percent of the weights, and only 13% and 3% of their directions rise above the simulated rounding floor.\nL255: Their very low stable rank therefore reflects a few real directions on top of rounding, and we draw no conclusion about preference training from them; our own DPO runs (§cite22†4.3 ) are the controlled test.\nL256: The specialists give a mixed picture. Against an instruct release of the same base, Qwen2.5-Coder-0.5B (stable rank 0.194) is more concentrated than Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (0.280), while Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B and Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B (0.200 and 0.191) are less concentrated than Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (0.051).\nL257: The specialists differ from the instruct models in both content and amount of training (their updates are about as large as the weights themselves), so this is not a controlled comparison; our matched runs in §cite22†4.3 are. What the released models show is that concentration varies with how much and what kind of training was done, and that nothing in it sets instruct updates apart.\nL258: ## 5 Case study: is post-training superficial?\nL259: cite51†Wu et al. (2025) compare released base and instruct models (Llama 3.1 and 3.2, cite94†Grattafiori et al., 2024 , and Qwen3) through the entropy effective rank (eRank, §cite7†2 ) of each weight matrix $W$ (not of $\\Delta W$). They find it almost unchanged by post-training, at about 85% of full rank, and read this as weight-level evidence that post-training is superficial.\nL260: They also propose an edit, Timber: keep the top $\\lceil\\mathrm{eRank}(\\Delta W)\\rceil$ singular directions of the update and scale the rest by $\\lambda<1$, with $\\lambda=0.2$ recommended, which improves Mean@$k$ and pass@$k$ on their benchmarks. We check both with the baselines of §cite7†2 .\nL261: ### 5.1 An unchanged effective rank is what any update of this size gives\nL262: Figure 4: Left: size of the change in the effective rank of the full weight matrix $W$, $|\\Delta\\,\\mathrm{eRank}(W)|/\\mathrm{eRank}(W)$ averaged over matrices, for the real base-to-instruct update (blue) and a random update of the same Frobenius norm (grey).\nL263: Right: perplexity on held-out wikitext of Qwen3-0.6B base, unchanged, after a random update of the same size as its real post-training update (Gaussian, or with the real update’s singular values), and after the real update, which gives the instruct model. The numbers above the bars give the size of the change in eRank($W$) for each update, as in the left panel.\nL264: The measurement reproduces. On Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen3-8B, two of their pairs, the effective rank of the base weights is 0.84 and 0.86 of full rank, and post-training changes it by only 0.13% and 0.06%.\nL265: But a random update of the same size also leaves it almost unchanged (Figure cite95†4 , left). On all seven base/instruct pairs, whose updates are 1–13% of the size of the weights, real and random updates both move the effective rank of $W$ by at most 0.5%. The real update moves it 1.6 to 3.8 times less than the random one, so post-training does change $W$ in a structured way, but that is visible only next to the random comparison.\nL266: Table 4: GSM8K accuracy, in percent (greedy, all 1,319 test problems), of Qwen3-0.6B with a random update of the same size as its real post-training update, added to every attention and MLP matrix. Each pair uses the same prompt and token limit: the base model an 8-shot plain-text prompt, the instruct model its chat template. Differences in points with 95% paired bootstrap intervals.\nL267: model  | accuracy  | difference  | $|$change$|$ in eRank($W$)\nL268: base  | 59.5  | –  | –\nL269: base + Gaussian update, draw 1  | 4.1  | $-55.4$ ($-58.2$, $-52.7$)  | 0.50%\nL270: base + Gaussian update, draw 2  | 4.8  | $-54.7$ ($-57.5$, $-52.2$)  | –\nL271: base + real singular values, random directions  | 5.8  | $-53.8$ ($-56.6$, $-50.9$)  | 0.48%\nL272: instruct (= base + real update)  | 64.5  | –  | 0.13%\nL273: instruct + Gaussian update  | 11.9  | $-52.6$ ($-55.3$, $-49.9$)  | –\nL274: An unchanged effective rank also says nothing about whether the model still works. Adding to Qwen3-0.6B base a random update of the same size as its real post-training update, matrix by matrix, moves its effective rank by 0.5%, but its GSM8K accuracy falls from 59.5% to 4–6%, whether the update is Gaussian or has exactly the real update’s singular values (Table cite96†4 ; details in Appendix cite46†G ). The same random update added to the instruct model takes it from 64.5% to 11.9%.\nL275: The real update, which moves the effective rank even less (0.13%), produces the instruct model. So an update can wreck a model or turn it into a working assistant and leave the effective rank of $W$ almost the same either way. Perplexity on wikitext rises from 18.1 to 33.1 with either random update (Figure cite95†4 , right), but the real update raises it too, to 50.1, because an instruct model no longer predicts plain web text well.\nL276: An unchanged effective rank therefore cannot tell a superficial change from a deep one, or from a destructive one. This does not show that post-training is deep; it shows that this measurement cannot decide the question.\nL277: ### 5.2 The edit does not need the effective-rank cutoff\nL278: Figure 5: GSM8K pass@$k$ for Qwen3-0.6B (non-thinking mode) with tail edits and with uniform scaling of the whole update. Eight samples per problem, the same sampling seed per problem for every variant. Left: first 300 test problems, main variants (every variant is in Table cite97†15 ). Right: all 1,319 (Table cite98†5 ). Table 5: Edits of the Qwen3-0.6B update on all 1,319 GSM8K test problems; pass@$k$ in percent.\nL279: “Unscaled” is the share of the update’s energy the edit leaves at full strength (uniform scaling shrinks every direction by the same factor); “answered” is the share of samples with a final boxed answer; “same size” means scaled to the size of the stable-rank edit. Differences from the unedited model in points, with 95% paired bootstrap intervals over problems, not corrected for multiple comparisons.\nL280: edit  | unscaled  | pass@1  | pass@8  | answered  | $\\Delta$ pass@1  | $\\Delta$ pass@8\nL281: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL282: none  | 100%  | 62.3  | 85.1  | 97.4%  | –  | –\nL283: tail $\\times 0.2$, eRank cutoff (Timber)  | 98.9%  | 62.3  | 85.3  | 97.1%  | $-0.0$ ($-0.8$, $+0.8$)  | $+0.2$ ($-1.4$, $+1.8$)\nL284: tail $\\times 0.5$, eRank cutoff (Timber)  | 98.9%  | 63.1  | 85.9  | 97.1%  | $+0.7$ ($-0.0$, $+1.6$)  | $+0.8$ ($-0.6$, $+2.3$)\nL285: tail $\\times 0.5$, stable-rank cutoff  | 41.4%  | 62.9  | 86.9  | 96.0%  | $+0.6$ ($-0.5$, $+1.7$)  | $+1.8$ ($+0.2$, $+3.5$)\nL286: whole update $\\times 0.75$ (same size)  | 0%  | 65.8  | 87.8  | 96.4%  | $+3.4$ ($+2.4$, $+4.5$)  | $+2.7$ ($+1.1$, $+4.2$)\nL287: Because the effective rank of $\\Delta W$ sits close to its noise value, the cutoff it gives is high: on Qwen3-0.6B it keeps 83% of the directions and 98.9% of the update’s energy, so the edit touches only the last 1% (Wu et al. report 98.82% for Qwen3-8B). An edit that small is also fragile in practice: running the edited model in bf16 distorts it by 16% of its size at $\\lambda=0.2$ and 25% at $\\lambda=0.5$ (Appendix cite47†H ).\nL288: We compare edits of the same update on two benchmarks: GSM8K (cite99†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), which is not among Wu et al.’s, and GPQA-Diamond (cite100†Rein et al., 2024 ), which is. On GSM8K we score with pass@$k$ (cite101†Chen et al., 2021 ) and compare: Timber’s tail edit, the same edit with a stable-rank cutoff that scales 53 times more of the update’s energy, and simply scaling the whole update by one factor per matrix (0.75 on average) so that the edited update has the same size as the stable-rank edit.\nL289: We run Timber’s edit both at its recommended $\\lambda=0.2$ and at $\\lambda=0.5$, and use Qwen3’s non-thinking mode, the mode of Wu et al.’s main table.\nL290: On all 1,319 problems, Timber’s edit has no detectable effect at either setting (Table cite98†5 , Figure cite102†5 ). At $\\lambda=0.2$ pass@1 is unchanged ($-0.0$ points; $-0.8$, $+0.8$) and pass@8 moves by $+0.2$ ($-1.4$, $+1.8$); at $\\lambda=0.5$ the changes are $+0.7$ ($-0.0$, $+1.6$) and $+0.8$ ($-0.6$, $+2.3$). The stable-rank edit gives a similar pass@1 and a pass@8 gain of $+1.8$ ($+0.2$, $+3.5$).\nL291: Simply scaling the whole update does better than either tail edit: pass@1 rises by $+3.4$ points ($+2.4$, $+4.5$) and pass@8 by $+2.7$ ($+1.1$, $+4.2$), with nearly the same share of answered samples (96.4% against 97.4%). It makes the update as small as the stable-rank edit does, but evenly from every direction instead of from the tail.\nL292: So on this benchmark the gain comes from making the update smaller, and choosing which directions to shrink by a spectral cutoff, whether eRank or stable rank, adds nothing. Task arithmetic already treats the scale of an update as a knob worth tuning (cite103†Ilharco et al., 2023 ); here that knob alone beats the spectral edits.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTrust but Verify: Designing Region-InhomogeneousForward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1)\nciteturn41263view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22380v1\",\"lineno\":215}); Total lines: 288\nL204: On clean conditioning, paired F1 deltas between treated arms and A straddle zero, judge NLL is identical to within 0.03 nats, and the held-out variational bound moves by $+0.0036$ nats/token (A 3.4072 vs. C/D weights 3.4108; gate $\\leq 0.02$), with all finetunes improving on the base checkpoint by $\\approx$0.35 nats through FineWeb-Edu adaptation. Removing the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture (arm E) is the one configuration that pays: persistently elevated clean-context cross-entropy (0.06 vs.\nL205: 0.03 nats), the largest bound delta ($+0.0071$), and the highest false-edit rate at the headline cell (1.82%). It is a more aggressive editor, at a measurable cost in calibration on clean input. Arm E differs from D in two ways, however: it drops the $\\eta{=}0$ component and raises the mean context-corruption rate (0.15 vs. 0.0375), so the comparison does not isolate the mixture itself; a matched-mean control is future work.\nL206: We also did not train a spatially homogeneous hybrid-noise arm, so the contrast with reports that such noise pays a downstream cost (cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ) is indirect.\nL207: #### Annealing (H4).\nL208: Moving from frozen-context read-out repair to the annealed sampler on the same weights raises plausible-corruption detection recall from 0.058 to 0.150 (uniform-trained C$\\to$D) and from 0.197 to 0.350 (hard-negative-trained CP$\\to$DP), at higher false-edit rates (0.26$\\to$0.83% and 0.31$\\to$1.28%). This comparison changes several factors at once: edit timing, the threshold (annealed from 0.5 vs. fixed at 0.1), the edit budget, and refilling by iterative reverse steps rather than a single read-out.\nL209: It therefore supports the sampler as a whole, not annealing specifically; isolating the schedule would require, for example, a fixed-threshold early-edit arm.\nL210: #### External-detector baseline.\nL211: On uniform corruptions, GPT-2 surprisal flagging is competitive but consistently behind: recall 0.90 vs. arm D’s 0.96 at $\\rho=0.1$, F1 0.297 vs. 0.304, judge NLL 2.701 vs. 2.610, with the gap widening at $\\rho=0.2$, where EXT recovers $+4.9$ F1 points over A against arm D’s $+6.4$. Against this detector the integrated detect–remask–refill loop is ahead even on conspicuous noise, although EXT receives a per-sequence edit budget matched to DP’s and refills with the same class of model.\nL212: On plausible corruptions the baseline fails structurally, with recall 0.003–0.076 and false-edit rates up to 10.4%: it flags rare-but-correct tokens, while the corruptions are, by construction, low-surprisal under left context. Its judge NLL of 3.101 lands well short of DP’s 2.902 against A’s 3.237. We hypothesize that detecting left-context-plausible corruptions requires bidirectional consistency checking, which the $\\eta$-finetuned model provides natively (DP: recall 0.350 at 1.28% false-edit).\nL213: We tested only this one detector, not bidirectional, larger, or trained ones, so the results do not show that external detectors in general are inferior.\nL214: #### The detectability–damage regularity.\nL215: Putting the pieces together: corruptions conspicuous enough to poison neighboring infills (uniform / out-of-distribution) are caught at 96% recall and their contamination is eliminated; corruptions that evade detection (locally plausible) are precisely those that damage infilling least.\nL216: We offer a simple stochastic interpretation, which we have not tested quantitatively (we do not measure the divergence directly): both detectability and damage may be governed by how far the corruption kernel departs from the data conditional $p_{\\mathrm{data}}(x^{i}\\mid\\text{context})$.\nL217: Plausible corruptions are near-samples from that conditional, and low divergence makes them simultaneously statistically invisible and semantically light; uniform-mixing noise sits far from it, making it at once conspicuous and destructive. Under this reading, the method closes the contamination gap where that divergence is large, and where exact-match gains are capped, the judge suggests that repair still improves passage-level quality.\nL218: The open question is the corruption class that is near the conditional and damaging, entity and number swaps, which our evaluation does not yet cover.\nL219: cite66†Image: Refer to caption L220: \nL221: cite67†Image: Refer to caption L222: Figure 2: Error containment at $\\rho=0.1$: infill token accuracy binned by distance to the nearest corrupted context token. Left (uniform): vanilla arms (base/A/B) dip to 0.088–0.11 adjacent to corruptions, below their own 0.14–0.16 at distance 3–4; $\\eta$-arms invert the dip (0.20–0.24 at distance 1–2), and curves converge by distance $\\geq 5$. Right (plausible): all arms coincide within noise at every distance; locally plausible substitutions barely contaminate neighboring infills.\nL223: ## 5 Discussion and limitations\nL224: The experiment points to a capability gap created by a collapsed forward-process design space. In our setting the gap narrows substantially for conspicuous corruptions when that space is reopened by composing two classical corruption kernels with region-dependent rates; this is cheap enough to run on a laptop and leaves clean-conditioning behavior intact. It narrows only slightly for plausible ones.\nL225: The two bracketing failure modes behaved as designed: over-editing is bounded by the inference budget and the false-edit gate (arm E is consistent with the $\\eta{=}0$ mixture also helping, subject to the confound noted above), while copy-collapse did not occur (detection recall well above chance in every treated arm).\nL226: The detectability–damage regularity sharpens what repair is for: not rescuing token-F1 on corruptions too close to the data conditional to matter locally, but eliminating the contamination that divergent corruptions cause, and improving passage-level coherence where a judge can see it.\nL227: Several directions follow from the dynamics view: the exact variational bound for region-inhomogeneous composed kernels (cite43†Campbell et al., 2022 ; cite50†von Rütte et al., 2025 ); per-position rates as a design surface (cite40†Chen et al., 2024 ); principled re-noising policies (threshold, annealing, budget) in place of our fixed heuristics; and combining context trust with ReMDM-style re-noising of committed tokens (cite52†Wang et al., 2025 ).\nL228: The limitations are plain: a single small scale (170M), one seed per arm, and a single text domain (English web text), so generality across sizes and domains is untested; gains under plausible corruption are small; the mixture and annealing ablations are confounded (Section cite23†4.2 ); the external-detector comparison uses one left-context detector; and the links to reverse-time dynamics and continuous inpainting are motivating analogies, not formal results.\nL229: Plausible corruptions come from one generator family; entity and number swaps (near-conditional yet damaging), code infilling with injected bugs, pairwise preference evaluation, and the deferred objective ablations are future work. The fixed budget $\\hat{\\rho}=0.15$ caps recall at high corruption rates ($\\approx$0.76 at $\\rho=0.2$).\nL230: Scaling to LLaDA-class models (cite48†Nie et al., 2025 ) remains open, as does the conjecture that known-region trust is a single design axis spanning discrete and continuous diffusion.\nL231: ## References\nL232:   * Anderson (1982) Brian D. O. Anderson. Reverse-time diffusion equation models. Stochastic Processes and their Applications, 12(3):313–326, 1982.\nL233:   * Arriola et al. (2025) Marianne Arriola, Aaron Gokaslan, Justin T. Chiu, Zhihan Yang, Zhixuan Qi, Jiaqi Han, Subham Sekhar Sahoo, and Volodymyr Kuleshov. Block diffusion: Interpolating between autoregressive and diffusion language models. In International Conference on Learning Representations, 2025.\nL234:   * Austin et al. (2021) Jacob Austin, Daniel D. Johnson, Jonathan Ho, Daniel Tarlow, and Rianne van den Berg. Structured denoising diffusion models in discrete state-spaces. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2021.\nL235:   * Campbell et al. (2022) Andrew Campbell, Joe Benton, Valentin De Bortoli, Thomas Rainforth, George Deligiannidis, and Arnaud Doucet. A continuous time framework for discrete denoising models. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2022.\nL236:   * Chang et al. (2022) Huiwen Chang, Han Zhang, Lu Jiang, Ce Liu, and William T. Freeman. MaskGIT: Masked generative image transformer. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, 2022.\nL237:   * Chen et al. (2024) Boyuan Chen, Diego Martí Monsó, Yilun Du, Max Simchowitz, Russ Tedrake, and Vincent Sitzmann. Diffusion forcing: Next-token prediction meets full-sequence diffusion. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2024.\nL238:   * Clark et al. (2020) Kevin Clark, Minh-Thang Luong, Quoc V. Le, and Christopher D. Manning. ELECTRA: Pre-training text encoders as discriminators rather than generators. In International Conference on Learning Representations, 2020.\nL239:   * Gokaslan and Cohen (2019) Aaron Gokaslan and Vanya Cohen. Openwebtext corpus. cite68†http://Skylion007.github.io/OpenWebTextCorpus†skylion007.github.io , 2019.\n"
